    sample: "Mostly because the alternative is exhausting. If everyone treated disagreement as permission, traffic, tax and contracts would depend on the mood of the nearest person. People also obey because they hope the law they dislike will protect them on a day when they are the weaker party. There is fear of a fine, of course, but fear does not explain the laws nobody is watching. Habit and a rough belief in reciprocity do more. The dangerous moment is when a large group stops believing the system will ever listen. Obedience then looks like naivety. A democracy needs room to disobey in public and argue, and a default of compliance while that argument is unfinished.",
